A||N||3||cscougar||Vacuum line question||||1||
Z||000000||cscougar||06-06-2001||12:44 PM||||My 2.8 V-6 idles with a slight miss to it.  I notice that by unplugging the vacuum line on the phenum by the distributor that the miss disappears but the idle increases.  Can anyone tell me where that vacuum line goes.  It disappears under the phenum and I can't tell where it connects on the other end.<P>Thanks<P>Ted||||reg||1
Z||000001||Carrolles||06-06-2001||02:01 PM||||There should be a sticker on the inside of the engine deck lid with a diagram showing the vacuum hose routing.  I don't remember off hand where that hose goes.<P>If you don't have the sticker I'll look at my car later.<P>BTW  The idle increases when you disconnect the hose from the plenum because you are introducing un-metered air into the plenum.  The O2 sensor tells the ECM that the engine is running lean and calls for more fuel from the injectors, hence the increased speed.<p>[This message has been edited by Carrolles (edited 06-06-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000002||DandRauto||06-06-2001||10:31 PM||||Did some once post a source to get these stickers either for replacement or for those that coverted L4 to V6s?||||reg||1
Z||000003||StuGood||06-07-2001||07:45 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by cscougar:<BR><B>...I notice that by unplugging the vacuum line on the phenum by the distributor that the miss disappears but the idle increases.  Can anyone tell me where that vacuum line goes...</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>The vacuum line I think you are talking about plugs into the side of the plenum near the distributor (usually has a little elbow fitting), is for the EGR actuating system.  The vacuum line runs under the plenum, to the EGR solenoid located on RHS of plenum, and another vac. line runs from there, back under the plenum, to the EGR valve.<P>So, if you disconnect that vac. line from the plenum, you do two things.  One, you disable the EGR system (which should also set a trouble code in the ECM). Two, if you leave that vac. line disconnected, you have created a vacuum leak, which increases the idle speed.    ||||reg||1
